  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 159 AM EDT Tue Sep 22 2026\n\nOver the next 48 hours a rex block pattern will take shape over the \neastern CONUS, with an upper low deepening over the Ohio valley and \ncentral Appalachian region and the upper high to its north \nstrengthening over the Canadian side of the Great Lakes region. \nMeanwhile, surface high pressure develops beneath the ridge over the \nGreat Lakes which will help to push a weak front, which was situated \nfrom West Virginia southwest into the Cumberland Plateau this \nevening, southward through the area sometime late Tuesday afternoon \nand evening. All of this will result in continued scattered to \nnumerous shower and thunderstorm activity across the region through \nlate Wednesday evening, with the best coverage during diurnally \nfavored daytime hours but also with lingering convection during the \novernight hours. For Tuesday, with the boundary slowly shifting \nsouth, the threat of any strong to severe storms should largely be \nrelegated to the far southern TN valley and our western NC counties. \nHowever, PWATs will remain elevated through Wednesday, and storm \nmotions very slow, so an isolated flooding threat could exist \nvirtually anywhere through that period, though in general the \nrisk of flooding should shift south with time as well.\n\nSpeaking of Wednesday, the pattern change really gets underway late \nWednesday. The upper low associated with the rex block pattern, \ndives east southeast to the coastal Carolina region Wednesday night \ninto Thursday, allowing for persistent northerly flow to spread \nacross the CWA, ushering in significantly cooler and drier air. \nWe'll still be warm on Wednesday, with highs in the mid to upper 80s \nsouth of I-40. But Thursday through the weekend will see highs \ntopping out largely in the 70s, except the far southern TN valley \nwhich may still manage to best the 80 degree mark each day. Perhaps \nthe more important note will be overnight lows in the 40s and 50s \nFriday night onward.",
